omniroute.evidence_risk:
HIGH if Claude Code is ever pointed at it. Every file Claude reads, including raw review notes in docs/research-log/data (some git-excluded on purpose), reproducers, before/after records and bench/out results, would be:
- copied into ~/.omniroute/call_logs, outside the repo and its exclude rules, kept for 90 days;
- forwarded to whichever upstream provider is selected, including free tiers and third-party clouds outside the trust boundary;
- possibly synced if cloud sync or a tunnel is enabled.
The 0.0.0.0 bind and the default CHANGEME dashboard password could also expose logged prompts to LAN peers through the dashboard; I did not verify which dashboard views show bodies. Compression and the PII masker change what the model reads, but OmniRoute does not modify the evidence files on disk. Current state: no evidence has leaked through it. No provider was connected, Claude Code was never pointed at it, and the server ran for about 69 seconds with zero requests ("Draining 0 request(s)"). The only outbound traffic was catalog and stat fetches (Arena, OpenRouter).

headroom.what_it_does:
(a) `headroom mcp serve` (cli/mcp.py) starts an MCP stdio server (ccr/mcp_server.py) with three tools:
- headroom_compress(content): wraps the content as one tool message, runs headroom.compress.compress() with the default lossy pipeline (CacheAligner, then ContentRouter: SmartCrusher for JSON arrays, Kompress ML for text, CodeCompressor needing tree-sitter, log and search compressors), stores the verbatim original, and returns the compressed text plus a hash.
- headroom_retrieve(hash): looks in the local store first, then POSTs to the proxy at http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1/retrieve.
- headroom_stats.
headroom_read (cached file reads) exists but is off unless HEADROOM_MCP_READ=on.
Originals go to the CCR store, by default an SQLite file at ~/.headroom/ccr_store.db (cache/compression_store.py _create_default_ccr_backend, cache/backends/sqlite.py). The file is mode 0600, holds at most 1000 entries with LRU eviction, and MCP entries have a 3600 s TTL. Expired rows are deleted.
Each compress call also:
- appends a counters-only event to ~/.headroom/session_stats.jsonl;
- writes a savings ledger entry;
- feeds the telemetry beacon, which mints ~/.headroom/config/install_id;
- probes http://127.0.0.1:8787/livez.
Every CLI start, including each `mcp serve` launch, runs a background PyPI version check (cli/main.py, update_check.py), at most once per 24 h. It only shows a notice; it does not auto-update. Updates happen only through an explicit `headroom update` or `uv tool upgrade`. No hooks, no skills, no daemon beyond the stdio MCP child.
The package can do far more that is not active here:
- a proxy (ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787) that rewrites every request's tool outputs, steers output verbosity, lowers thinking effort, and injects memory;
- `headroom wrap claude`, which starts the proxy and registers a Serena MCP server in ~/.claude.json;
- `headroom learn`, which mines past sessions and writes CLAUDE.local.md or CLAUDE.md;
- `headroom mcp install`, which writes its config into every detected agent.
(b) A skill (SKILL.md), a /headroom:peek command and one stdlib-only script, scripts/peek.py. The script prints a file's size and line count plus an outline: Markdown headings with line numbers, the 12 most frequent log-line patterns with digits masked and the first line of each, the first two levels of a JSON file's structure (refuses files over 200 MB), or the CSV header and 3 sample rows. The skill tells Claude to read line ranges, grep logs, use jq or python for JSON, count and sum CSV data with code, and keep a digest file (example: notes/<file>.digest.md) with line references. No hooks, MCP, network or background processes.

headroom.evidence_risk:
(a) No MCP tool here can modify or delete files in the repo:
- compress and retrieve operate on strings;
- headroom_read is disabled and read-only anyway.
Every compressed item is copied verbatim into ~/.headroom/ccr_store.db and stays up to 1 h. That is a second copy of review notes or reproducer output outside the repo's git-exclude handling, on the same machine. The beacon uploads only counters about evidence (token sizes, content-type and strategy slugs) with a persistent install_id to a Headroom Labs Cloudflare endpoint. That is metadata, not content, but it is a third-party outbound path the user never chose.
Not installed or not enabled, and must stay that way:
- HEADROOM_KOMPRESS_ENDPOINT, which would send raw content off-box;
- proxy and wrap, which would see and rewrite all traffic, including evidence read into context;
- `headroom learn`, which reads Claude Code session transcripts and writes CLAUDE.local.md or CLAUDE.md into the repo.
The main evidence risk is misreporting (a lossy view quoted as fact), not deletion.
(b) peek.py only reads the file it is given. The skill's suggested digest (notes/<file>.digest.md) would be created by Claude in the working tree, so a digest of git-excluded review notes could end up committed. No upload or delete path.
